<handout>
<title>Simplifying Rational Expressions</title>
<page>
    <objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to simplify rational expressions by:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Reducing numerical fractions
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Canceling out common fractions.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Noting when the denominator is zero.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    A <term>rational expression</term> is a ratio of polynomials with variables in both the numerator and denominator. Examples of rational expressions are 
    <md>
        \frac{30x^2}{5x}
    </md>
    and
    <md>
        \frac{4x^2+3x-1}{2x-8}
    </md>
    A rational expression is undefined when the denominator is zero. For example, <m>\frac{30x^2}{5x}</m> is undefined when <m>x=0</m> because inserting <m>x=0</m> into the 
    expression makes the denominator equal zero. 
</p>
<p>
    Rational expressions may be simplified by canceling out common factors from the numerator and denominator. For example <m>\frac{30x^2}{5x}</m> may be written in factored
    form as <m>\frac{5\cdot 6 \cdot x \cdot x}{5\cdot x}</m>. There are a 5 and and <m>x</m> common to both the numerator and denominator, we may cancel them out. But we need
    to be careful. For while <m>\frac{30}{5} = 6</m> but <m>\frac{x^2}{x}</m> does not exactly equal <m>x</m> because <m>x</m> can be any number but <m>\frac{x^2}{x}</m> does
    not allow <m>x=0</m>. So when we simplify <m>\frac{30x^2}{5x} = 6x</m> we also have to say <m>x\ne 0</m>.
</p>
<p>
    So there are four steps to simplifying a rational expression:
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            Factor the numerator and denominator.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
           Reduce numerical factors.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Cancel out common factors with variables.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            State which values of the variable(s) make the denominator zero.
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
</p>
</page>
<page>
    <example workspace="2.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
             Simplify the expression <m>\frac{5x+50}{30}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{x+10}{6}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Factor the numerator and denominator to get \frac{5(x+10)}{5\cdot 6}. Cancel out the common 5's to get <m>\frac{x+10}{6}</m>. Since we only canceled out
                numbers, we do not need to state when the denominator is zero.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example workspace="2.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Simplify the expression <m>\frac{6}{3x+27}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{2}{x+9}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Factor the numerator and denominator to get \frac{3\cdot 2}{3(x+9)}. Cancel out the common 2's to get <m>\frac{2}{x+9}</m>. Since we only canceled out
                numbers, we do not need to state when the denominator is zero.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example workspace="2.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Simplify the expression <m>\frac{2x-10}{x^2-8x+15}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{2}{x-3}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Factor the numerator and denominator to get \frac{2(x-5)}{(x-3)(x-5)}. Cancel out the common <m>(x-5)</m> to get <m>\frac{2}{x-3}</m>. 
                Since we canceled out <m>x-5</m>, we need to state that <m>x\ne 5</m> since <m>x=5</m> make the denominator zero.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
</page>
<page>
    <example workspace="2.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Simplify the expression <m>\frac{x^2+4x-21}{x^2+11x+28}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{x-3}{x+4}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Factor the numerator and denominator to get \frac{(x-3)(x+7)}{(x+4)(x+7)}. Cancel out the common <m>(x+7)</m> to get <m>\frac{x-3}{x+4}</m>. 
                Since we canceled out <m>x+7</m>, we need to state that <m>x\ne -7</m> since <m>x=-7</m> make the denominator zero.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example workspace="2.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Simplify the expression <m>\frac{4x^2+3x-1}{4x^2-13x+3}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{x+1}{x-3}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Factor the numerator and denominator to get \frac{(x+1)(4x-1)}{(x-3)(4x-1)}. Cancel out the common <m>(4x-1)</m> to get <m>\frac{x+1}{x-3}</m>. 
                Since we canceled out <m>4x-1</m>, we need to state that <m>x\ne \frac{1}{4}</m> since <m>x=\frac{1}{4}</m> make the denominator zero.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    <example workspace="2.0in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Simplify the expression <m>\frac{x^2+11x+30}{x^2-36}</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                <m>\frac{x+5}{x-6}</m>
            </p>
        </answer>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Factor the numerator and denominator to get \frac{(x+6)(x+5)}{(x+6)(x-6)}. Cancel out the common <m>(x+6)</m> to get <m>\frac{x+5}{x-6}</m>. 
                Since we canceled out <m>x+6</m>, we need to state that <m>x\ne -6</m> since <m>x=-6</m> make the denominator zero.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
</page>
<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Try it Now!</title>
      <p>
        Simplify the following expressions.
    </p>  
    <p workspace="1.0in">
      (a) <m>\frac{x+2}{x^2+5x+6}</m> 
    </p>
    <p workspace="1.0in">
     (b) <m>\frac{x^2-x-2}{x^2-3x+2}</m> 
    </p>
    <p workspace="1.0in">
     (c) <m>\frac{x^2-3x+10}{x^2+x-2}</m> 
    </p>
    <p workspace="1.0in">
     (d) <m>\frac{x^2-4x-32}{64-x^2}</m> 
    </p>
    <p workspace="1.0in">
     (e) <m>\frac{x^2-4x-5}{25-x^2}</m> 
    </p>
    </paragraphs>
    
</page>
</handout>